WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
<v The Announcer>On this season of LAiDIES: she stops
feeling behind.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.380
<v The Announcer>She learns to delegate to machines —
without lowering her standards.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.640
<v The Announcer>She builds her own little squad of AI
helpers.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.940
<v The Announcer>And she becomes the woman other people
come to when the future gets confusing.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.560
<v The Announcer>And on this episode: our heroine finally
stops putting it off.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.860
<v The Announcer>This is Episode One: On Wednesdays We
Do AI.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.780
<v The Heroine>It's 4:52 on a Tuesday afternoon, and a
man named Steve is being called a
visionary. Steve works in revenue
operations.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.340
<v The Heroine>Steve has never once refilled the
printer.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.650
<v The Heroine>And Steve has just presented a
competitive analysis — clean, confident,

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:45.261
<v The Heroine>suspiciously well-formatted — that
everyone in that room knows took him

00:00:45.261 --> 00:00:49.780
<v The Heroine>about an hour, because at lunch he was
playing pickleball.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.120
<v The Heroine>I know because he told us. Twice. And
me?

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:02.160
<v The Heroine>I had a version of that same analysis.
Mine took two weekends. Mine had
footnotes.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:08.200
<v The Heroine>Mine was still sitting in my drafts
folder, waiting until it felt... ready.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.480
<v The Heroine>Somewhere between Steve's standing
ovation and my fourth coffee, it hit me.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.940
<v The Heroine>He isn't smarter than me.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.920
<v The Heroine>He isn't better informed.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.300
<v The Heroine>He just stopped doing it the hard way.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.140
<v The Heroine>And I couldn't help but wonder... when
did everyone learn to do that?

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.620
<v The Heroine>And when, exactly, was I supposed to?

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.969
<v The Heroine>Welcome to LAiDIES — where smart, busy
women learn AI one Wednesday at a time,

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:41.920
<v The Heroine>from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.820
<v The Heroine>New here? check out the trailer first.
It's the tour.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.720
<v The Heroine>This is where the story starts.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.020
<v The Heroine>And that "she" from the promo? That's
me.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.341
<v The Heroine>I'm telling this from a few steps ahead
— I've made the mistakes, done the

00:01:59.341 --> 00:02:03.921
<v The Heroine>reading, and kept the receipts — and as
the season goes on, you'll hear from

00:02:03.921 --> 00:02:09.780
<v The Heroine>other women figuring it out in real
time. This isn't a lecture. It's a group
chat.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.113
<v The Heroine>So if Steve sounded familiar — if you
have ever sat on work that was better

00:02:16.113 --> 00:02:20.940
<v The Heroine>than what got the applause — get in
loser. We're learning AI...

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:31.220
<v The Heroine>So. Let me tell you what I did after the
Steve meeting. Nothing. For six months.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.100
<v The Heroine>Well — not nothing.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.225
<v The Heroine>I did what every competent woman does
with a problem she doesn't have time

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.680
<v The Heroine>for: I put it on a list.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.280
<v The Heroine>And in my defense, every time I tried to
start, the on-ramp was terrible.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:53.922
<v The Heroine>Everything I found was either written by
men in fleece vests — say no more — or

00:02:53.922 --> 00:03:01.080
<v The Heroine>so surface-level it basically amounted
to "AI is transformative!" AI is
transformative?

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.000
<v The Heroine>Groundbreaking. Here's the thing. I have
a full-time job.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.580
<v The Heroine>I manage a team.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.620
<v The Heroine>My calendar is a Tetris game I am
perpetually losing.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.014
<v The Heroine>So the idea of adding "become
AI-literate" to that pile felt about as

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:23.880
<v The Heroine>realistic as Miranda Priestly asking me
to fetch the unpublished Harry Potter
manuscript.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.700
<v The Heroine>Technically possible — but at what
personal cost?

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:35.194
<v The Heroine>Then one night — eleven p.m., everyone
else asleep, glass of wine — I went

00:03:35.194 --> 00:03:37.300
<v The Heroine>looking for a different answer.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.180
<v The Heroine>And somewhere past the third search
result... I found a town.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.974
<v The Heroine>An actual little internet town,
permanently set in nineteen ninety-nine,

00:03:47.974 --> 00:03:52.580
<v The Heroine>with a main street, a radio station, and
a library that never closes.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.240
<v The Heroine>It's that kind of place.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.660
<v The Heroine>I didn't know it yet, but I was moving
in.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:05.907
<v The Heroine>And that first night, in that library,
the question I brought wasn't "how do I

00:04:05.907 --> 00:04:08.600
<v The Heroine>learn AI." It was: is it just me?

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.640
<v The Heroine>It was not just me.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:13.860
<v The Heroine>I found a Harvard study.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.480
<v The Heroine>A hundred and forty thousand people,
around the world.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.360
<v The Heroine>For every one hundred men using
generative AI tools... seventy-eight
women.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:30.440
<v The Heroine>And that gap holds even when you control
for job type, age, education, and
access.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.580
<v The Heroine>Same tools. Same desks. Different
adoption rates.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.460
<v The Heroine>And the deeper I read, the worse it got.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.420
<v The Heroine>Men are twenty-three percent more likely
to be encouraged by their managers to
use AI.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.800
<v The Heroine>Twenty-seven percent more likely to be
praised when they do.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.480
<v The Heroine>Steve got called a visionary, remember.
And women?

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:01.500
<v The Heroine>We're thirty-two percent more likely to
worry that using AI looks like cutting
corners.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.640
<v The Heroine>That one hit me like Samantha Jones
delivering an uncomfortable truth over
brunch.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.200
<v The Heroine>Because of course we worry about that.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.318
<v The Heroine>We've spent entire careers building
credibility through preparation,

00:05:16.318 --> 00:05:18.200
<v The Heroine>thoroughness, visible effort.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:24.766
<v The Heroine>And now there's a tool that makes hard
things look easy — and some part of our

00:05:24.766 --> 00:05:31.460
<v The Heroine>brain calculates, correctly, based on
historical data, that "easy" might get
held against us.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.240
<v The Heroine>That's not imposter syndrome.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.240
<v The Heroine>That's pattern recognition.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.020
<v The Heroine>Which is ironic, given what AI actually
is — but we'll get there.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.799
<v The Heroine>You know how there's always one person
who preps the deck before the meeting,

00:05:47.799 --> 00:05:52.280
<v The Heroine>remembers the feedback from last time,
follows up on the action items nobody

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:55.480
<v The Heroine>else tracked — and still delivers her
own work on time?

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.760
<v The Heroine>That person is usually not named Steve.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.500
<v The Heroine>No offense to the Steves out there
fighting the good fight.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.900
<v The Heroine>So no — this was never a confidence
problem. It's a physics problem.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.780
<v The Heroine>You cannot add hours to a day that's
already over-subscribed.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.463
<v The Heroine>And the irony is, the tool that could
give you your time back requires time

00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:24.480
<v The Heroine>you don't have to learn it. So you don't
start.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.864
<v The Heroine>And the gap compounds, week over week,
until the distance between you and the

00:06:30.864 --> 00:06:36.480
<v The Heroine>colleague who started six months ago
isn't a gap anymore. It's a canyon.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:44.320
<v The Heroine>As Dolly would say... You'd better get
to building your own bridge, honey —
because ain't nobody building it for
you.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:50.170
<v The Heroine>One more thing I found that night, from
Fei-Fei Li — the Stanford professor they

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:53.700
<v The Heroine>call the Godmother of AI. She said it
plainly:

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:59.041
<v The Heroine>If we don't get women involved in AI,
we're going to have a future built by

00:06:59.041 --> 00:07:01.820
<v The Heroine>half the population — for all of the
population.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.120
<v The Heroine>And it's not just the principle of the
thing.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:10.975
<v The Heroine>When women don't use these tools, the
tools learn from a skewed pool of users

00:07:10.975 --> 00:07:13.420
<v The Heroine>— they literally get worse for women.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.100
<v The Heroine>And around the cycle goes.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.480
<v The Heroine>So this was never just about my career,
or yours.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:27.680
<v The Heroine>So here's what finally got me to start.
It wasn't inspiration. It was spite.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:34.723
<v The Heroine>Fine — it was mostly spite, plus a
Sunday morning at the Blend and Snap —

00:07:34.723 --> 00:07:40.707
<v The Heroine>my corner table, an oat latte going
cold, the radio on low — fifteen quiet

00:07:40.707 --> 00:07:43.020
<v The Heroine>minutes, and nothing to lose.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.900
<v The Heroine>There was an email I'd been avoiding for
four days.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:53.880
<v The Heroine>You know the kind — the delicate one, to
the stakeholder who reads tone into line
breaks.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:08:00.165
<v The Heroine>I opened one of the AI tools, and
instead of asking it something grand, I

00:08:00.165 --> 00:08:05.532
<v The Heroine>just told it the truth: who the email
was for, what I needed from them, and

00:08:05.532 --> 00:08:07.580
<v The Heroine>what I couldn't say out loud.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.000
<v The Heroine>It gave me a draft in nine seconds.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.320
<v The Heroine>And it was... eighty percent right.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.764
<v The Heroine>The other twenty percent was wrong in
ways only I could see — which, it turns

00:08:21.764 --> 00:08:23.260
<v The Heroine>out, is the good news.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.640
<v The Heroine>I fixed it with my own judgment. I hit
send.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:34.340
<v The Heroine>And then I looked at the clock. Four
days of dread. Eleven minutes of work.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.920
<v The Heroine>And that's when it landed: oh. I can do
this.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:45.100
<v The Heroine>Because the thing stopping me was never
ability.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.860
<v The Heroine>It was that nobody had explained it in a
way that made me want to start.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.280
<v The Heroine>Now — I want to be clear.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.120
<v The Heroine>I am no AI slayer.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:09:01.820
<v The Heroine>There's no Watcher guiding me through
some prophecy, no training montage that
happened off-screen.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:07.900
<v The Heroine>I'm still learning, still getting things
wrong, still googling things
mid-conversation.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.320
<v The Heroine>But here's my favourite stat I've found
so far, and it's the one that keeps me
going.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.820
<v The Heroine>BCG looked at senior women in tech — the
ones who push through that first awkward
phase.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:21.840
<v The Heroine>And they're not trailing the men.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.081
<v The Heroine>They're out in front — already using
these tools more than their male peers,

00:09:27.081 --> 00:09:30.120
<v The Heroine>by fourteen percentage points. Fourteen.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.184
<v The Heroine>Not because they had some head start —
but because they were more aware it

00:09:36.184 --> 00:09:38.380
<v The Heroine>mattered, and more willing to take the
leap.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.820
<v The Heroine>You don't claw your way to senior
without learning to bet on yourself.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.840
<v The Heroine>And what they bring to it is the one
thing AI cannot replicate: a career's

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.940
<v The Heroine>worth of professional judgment.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.820
<v The Heroine>The instinct that something is off in a
document before you can even articulate
why.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.700
<v The Heroine>That twenty percent of my email only I
could see?

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.980
<v The Heroine>That's your critical thinking.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.760
<v The Heroine>Which brings me to my favourite part of
every episode.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:11.648
<v The Heroine>We call it the cocktail party
explanation, and it was born at the

00:10:11.648 --> 00:10:17.000
<v The Heroine>Bronze Age — that's our bar in
SUNNYVAiLE — during businesswomen's
special happy hour.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.427
<v The Heroine>I was three sips into telling my
girlfriends about the email thing when

00:10:22.427 --> 00:10:26.440
<v The Heroine>one of them put down her drink and said,
"okay, but what IS it?

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:31.860
<v The Heroine>Actually?" The table went quiet.
Everybody wanted to know.

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.540
<v The Heroine>Nobody wanted to be the one to ask.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:40.861
<v The Heroine>So now, every week, this segment hands
you the answer you'll give the next time

00:10:40.861 --> 00:10:45.962
<v The Heroine>somebody at a dinner table — or a happy
hour — says "AI" like they know what

00:10:45.962 --> 00:10:47.420
<v The Heroine>they're talking about.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.560
<v The Heroine>This week's question is the big one:
what is this thing, actually?

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.760
<v The Heroine>And I'll give you exactly what I gave
the girls.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.380
<v The Heroine>It's the most talented new hire you'll
ever manage. That's it.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:08.036
<v The Heroine>It fits in the lull before the drinks
arrive, and it holds up under every

00:11:08.036 --> 00:11:10.380
<v The Heroine>follow-up — so let me show you why.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.653
<v The Heroine>A brilliant new hire has read
everything: books, articles, forums,

00:11:16.653 --> 00:11:22.622
<v The Heroine>manuals, billions of documents — it's
absorbed how language works, how ideas

00:11:22.622 --> 00:11:25.180
<v The Heroine>connect, how arguments get built.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.980
<v The Heroine>That's why it sounds so impressive.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:34.488
<v The Heroine>And it has lived nothing: no job, no
relationships, no consequences, not one

00:11:34.488 --> 00:11:39.620
<v The Heroine>awkward moment at a holiday party that
taught it something about people.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.120
<v The Heroine>So the honest picture isn't a robot
genius, and it isn't a toy.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:52.226
<v The Heroine>It's day-one talent: superhuman range,
astonishing speed, first drafts that'll

00:11:52.226 --> 00:11:58.255
<v The Heroine>genuinely scare you — and no read on
your office politics, no stake in what

00:11:58.255 --> 00:12:02.260
<v The Heroine>happens if it's wrong. That's where you
come in.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.200
<v The Heroine>You don't hand it the keys — you onboard
it.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:10.760
<v The Heroine>You give it guidance and guardrails, the
lay of the land around the office.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.240
<v The Heroine>You manage it, and you review its work.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.020
<v The Heroine>And you've done this all before.

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.883
<v The Heroine>Now — under the hood, when you type
something into ChatGPT, or Claude, or

00:12:23.883 --> 00:12:29.840
<v The Heroine>Gemini, what it's doing is prediction —
generating what comes next, word by
word.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.700
<v The Heroine>And here's the strange part: it's still
just that, underneath.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:41.085
<v The Heroine>But what grew out of all that prediction
— it drafts, it reasons, it'll even run

00:12:41.085 --> 00:12:45.639
<v The Heroine>multi-step errands for you — is
impressive enough that the experts

00:12:45.639 --> 00:12:51.620
<v The Heroine>genuinely can't agree on what to call
it, or even whether "reasoning" is the
right word.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.340
<v The Heroine>We'll get to all of that later in the
season.

00:12:55.220 --> 00:13:00.567
<v The Heroine>But two limits are not up for debate
(and yes, in this case the limits DO

00:13:00.567 --> 00:13:06.420
<v The Heroine>exist) — and they're the two that decide
whether it helps you or embarrasses you.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:12.000
<v The Heroine>Limit one: it does not know your context
until you hand it over.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:18.426
<v The Heroine>It's Cher's closet computer from
Clueless: endless outfit combinations,

00:13:18.426 --> 00:13:23.394
<v The Heroine>all day long — but it has no idea the
meeting is with a conservative client

00:13:23.394 --> 00:13:28.100
<v The Heroine>who is already looking for reasons to
dismiss you... unless you tell it.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:35.160
<v The Heroine>Remember my email? I told it the truth
about who it was for, and it used every
word of that.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.720
<v The Heroine>The context lives with you.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:43.480
<v The Heroine>Handing it over well — that's a skill,
and it's literally next week's episode.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.280
<v The Heroine>Limit two: out of the box, it does not
check whether what it says is true.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:52.440
<v The Heroine>Only whether it's plausible.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:58.531
<v The Heroine>It's the Burn Book from Mean Girls: some
entries accurate, some completely

00:13:58.531 --> 00:14:02.740
<v The Heroine>fabricated, all delivered with the same
unbothered confidence.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:10.520
<v The Heroine>"Made out with a hot dog"? Same
certainty as an actual fact — it has no
way of telling the two apart.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:14.500
<v The Heroine>Regina George energy. But make it AI.

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:20.154
<v The Heroine>Three words before you go, so you don't
pull a Cher, confidently arguing it

00:14:20.154 --> 00:14:23.080
<v The Heroine>doesn't say R.S.V.P. on the Statue of
Liberty.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:30.220
<v The Heroine>Generative AI: the kind that makes new
things — text, images, first drafts.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.386
<v The Heroine>A Carrie Bradshaw in your laptop — not
one that finds you articles to read,

00:14:36.386 --> 00:14:40.000
<v The Heroine>that's Google — one that writes the
column for you.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.800
<v The Heroine>Model: the trained brain behind the app.

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:50.311
<v The Heroine>ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini are the
magazines; the models are their

00:14:50.311 --> 00:14:54.940
<v The Heroine>editors-in-chief — different taste,
different judgment, different style.

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.500
<v The Heroine>Same brief, wildly different results.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:06.217
<v The Heroine>And hallucination: when the answer comes
back polished, confident... and partly

00:15:06.217 --> 00:15:08.020
<v The Heroine>or completely made up.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.460
<v The Heroine>It's not lying — lying takes intent.
It's guessing.

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:17.992
<v The Heroine>The newer models are better at it —
they'll flag when they're unsure, and if

00:15:17.992 --> 00:15:21.000
<v The Heroine>you hand them a source, they'll actually
check it.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:26.934
<v The Heroine>But better isn't fixed: push one past
what it really knows and it'll still

00:15:26.934 --> 00:15:28.880
<v The Heroine>answer with total authority.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:34.960
<v The Heroine>Think of your most confident friend,
who'll answer any question whether or
not she really knows.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.880
<v The Heroine>I'm not a regular mom — I'm a cool mom.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:44.320
<v The Heroine>That's AI trying to be helpful and
completely misreading the room.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:50.840
<v The Heroine>And your job, in all of this, is knowing
which parts to trust and which to push
back on.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:56.206
<v The Heroine>Which — good news — is exactly what
you've been doing with other people's

00:15:56.206 --> 00:16:01.980
<v The Heroine>work your entire career, and we're going
to help you apply those skills to AI
over the season.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.060
